  <h1>Architecture model making basics</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">A physical model tests your design in three dimensions &amp; sells it in a crit. Good technique makes the difference between a study tool &amp; a mess of glue.</p>

  <p>Model making is a core studio skill. A model lets you understand light, mass, proportion &amp; structure in a way a screen never will, &amp; a clean model reads instantly at a review. You do not need expensive kit &mdash; you need the right material, a sharp blade &amp; patience.</p>

  <h2>Materials</h2>
  <p>Choose your material for the stage of the project &amp; the effect you want. Working (massing) models can be quick &amp; rough; final models reward care.</p>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Mount / card</td><td>Fast massing studies, folded forms, cheap iteration</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Foamboard</td><td>Walls &amp; floors with thickness; quick, lightweight structures</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Basswood / balsa</td><td>Structure, framing, refined final models with real grain</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Acrylic / acetate</td><td>Glazing, water, transparent volumes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>MDF / plywood</td><td>Robust bases &amp; heavier context</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Basswood cuts more cleanly than balsa &amp; holds an edge better, which is why it is the studio favourite for finished work. Foamboard is fast but its foam core shows at cut edges, so bevel or cap them on a display model.</p>

  <h2>Tools &amp; safety</h2>
  <p>Your essentials are a scalpel or snap-off knife with fresh blades, a steel rule (never plastic &mdash; a blade will shave it), a self-healing cutting mat, a set square &amp; PVA plus a glue that suits your material. Change blades often: a blunt blade tears &amp; slips, which is how most injuries happen. Always cut away from your body, keep fingers behind the steel rule, &amp; take several light passes rather than forcing one deep cut.</p>

  <h2>Choosing a scale</h2>
  <p>Match the model scale to what you want to show. 1:200 or 1:500 suits a site or massing model; 1:100 &amp; 1:50 suit a building; 1:20 &amp; larger suit a detail or bay study. Decide early, because scale governs how much detail is legible &mdash; window frames that read at 1:20 vanish at 1:200, so do not waste effort on detail the scale cannot carry.</p>

  <h2>Cutting cleanly</h2>
  <p>Accuracy is everything: a model is only as square as its cuts. Mark lightly in pencil, hold the steel rule firmly on the piece you are keeping, &amp; let the blade follow the rule in repeated light strokes. For thick foamboard, score the top skin, cut the core, then release the back skin. Cut all identical pieces from one template so they truly match.</p>

  <h2>Gluing</h2>
  <p>Use the least glue that will hold. PVA is the general-purpose choice for card &amp; wood: apply a thin bead, hold or pin until tacky. Spray adhesive gives flat, even bonds for laminating sheets. Solvent cement welds acrylic. Superglue is fast but brittle &amp; can fog clear surfaces. Keep a damp cloth to hand &amp; wipe squeeze-out immediately &mdash; dried glue catches the light &amp; ruins a clean face.</p>

  <h2>Base &amp; context</h2>
  <p>A base lifts a model from a pile of parts into a considered object. Give it a clean, square edge, keep it neutral so it does not compete with the building, &amp; show enough context &mdash; neighbouring footprints, roads, a slice of landscape &mdash; to explain the site. Contours can be built up in layered card to model a slope.</p>

  <h2>Photographing models</h2>
  <p>Photograph against a plain, seamless background in soft, even light &mdash; near a window or under diffused lamps avoids harsh shadows. Shoot from eye level for a realistic view &amp; from above for the plan relationship. A small aperture keeps more of the model sharp. Good photos let a model live on in your portfolio long after the crit.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Build a quick, ugly test piece of any tricky junction first. Ten minutes spent failing on scrap saves you from ruining the real component.</div>
